<Context title="Explaining textual differences to churches and reviewers" label="Guide" lastChecked="2026-11-25" sources={sources}
  description="What to say when readers or reviewers ask why a verse is missing or a word has changed: the evidence, the project's policy, and short sample answers.">
  <p class="lead">Sooner or later a pastor or a reviewer asks: "Why is this verse missing?" or "Why did you change this word?" The question is fair. This page helps you answer it plainly, with the evidence and your project's policy, without attacking anyone's Bible.</p>

  <h2>Before you answer</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li><strong>Ask which verse.</strong> Find out which verse, and which Bible the person reads.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that it is textual.</strong> Some differences come from translation, not from the Greek text. <a href={url('learn/decisions/compare-versions/')}>Part 1: Compare versions</a> shows how to tell the two apart.</li>
    <li><strong>Look at their Bible's own note.</strong> Several Indian-language Bibles already mark doubtful verses. The Hindi Old Version puts twelve doubtful verses in brackets with a footnote. You can start from the note their own Bible prints.</li>
    <li><strong>Know your project's decision.</strong> If the Review Committee has already decided the verse, explain that decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start from the Bible they know</h2>
  <p>Most Indian-language Bibles checked so far stand mainly on the side of the <a href={g('tr')}>Textus Receptus</a> (TR), the printed Greek text behind the King James Version. A new translation made from the UBS Greek New Testament will differ from them in a known set of places. Nobody has made a mistake. The two Bibles come from two different Greek texts. See <a href={url('guide/base-texts/')}>Two Greek texts behind our Bibles</a>.</p>
  <p>If you give some history, keep it to a few dates:</p>
  <ol class="mini-line">
    <li><span class="yr">1516</span>Erasmus prints the first published Greek New Testament, from a few late manuscripts.</li>
    <li><span class="yr">1633</span>An Elzevir edition calls its text the one "received by all". From this comes the name Textus Receptus for this line of editions. Earlier editions in the line lie behind the King James Version (1611).</li>
    <li><span class="yr">Since then</span>Much older manuscripts have come to light, some from the 3rd and 4th centuries.</li>
    <li><span class="yr">1966</span>The first UBS Greek New Testament, made for Bible translators. Its text takes the older manuscripts into account.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="small muted">From the timeline in <a href={url('background/nt-text/')}>How we got the New Testament text</a> and the reasons in <a href={url('guide/text-policy/#why-the-critical-text')}>Which text should a translation follow?</a></p>

  <h2>Explain the policy, not your opinion</h2>
  <p>Give the project's policy, not your own view. The <a href={url('guide/text-policy/')}>text policy</a> on this site says:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The New Testament is translated from the UBS or Nestle–Aland critical text.</li>
    <li>Where a passage has a long tradition in the regional-language Bible, follow that Bible. This keeps the translation acceptable to the wider church and keeps the churches united.</li>
    <li>The <a href={url('guide/text-policy/#rc')}>Review Committee</a>, church leaders from the community, decides the base text and any passage where the regional-language Bible and the UBS text differ.</li>
    <li>Theology never changes the text, in either direction.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>So you can honestly say: "This is not one translator's choice. Our Review Committee looked at it, following a written policy." Say this only if it is true for your project.</p>
  <p>If your project follows another organisation's guidance, explain that guidance instead.</p>

  <h2>Footnotes are the bridge</h2>
  <p>A footnote lets readers see both readings. If the text follows the UBS reading, the footnote gives the reading of their older Bible. If the committee keeps the familiar reading, the footnote says what the earliest manuscripts have. Either way, nothing is hidden. See <a href={url('guide/footnotes/')}>Writing textual footnotes</a>.</p>

  <h2>Sample answers</h2>
  <p>These are short answers built from the evidence on this site. Put them in your own words and in your own language. Change them to match what your Review Committee decided.</p>

  <section class="qa">
    <h3>"Why is Acts 8:37 missing?"</h3>
    <div class="ans">
      <p>"Your Bible follows a Greek text printed in the 1500s, the Textus Receptus. Our draft follows the UBS Greek New Testament, which uses many older manuscripts found since then. The UBS editors judged that this verse was not part of what Luke first wrote. So they give it in their notes, not in the main text.</p>
      <p>Many modern Bibles keep the verse number and put the words in a footnote, so verse numbers still match. Our Review Committee will decide how we print it."</p>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">Facts from <a href={url('guide/base-texts/')}>Two Greek texts</a> and <a href={url('guide/doubtful-passages/')}>Doubtful passages</a>. The same pattern of answer fits the other whole verses on that list: two Greek texts, the verse number kept, a footnote, and the Review Committee decides. The evidence for each verse is different, so check it before you speak about one verse.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa">
    <h3>"Why is 1 John 5:7 different?"</h3>
    <div class="ans">
      <p>"In the King James Version, verse 7 speaks of three that bear witness 'in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost'. These words are not in the early Greek manuscripts. Almost no Greek manuscript has them.</p>
      <p>Erasmus printed the Greek New Testament in 1516 without them. He said he had found no Greek manuscript that had them. Later a Greek manuscript with the words was produced, probably written about 1520, and he added them in his third edition of 1522. From there they passed into the Textus Receptus and the King James Version. Scholars who studied that manuscript say its scribe took the words from the Latin Bible.</p>
      <p>Our policy says that where a passage has a long tradition in our regional-language Bible, the Review Committee decides how to print it. Whichever way it decides, a footnote will show the other form."</p>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">Facts from the micro module <a href={url('practice/1-john-5-7/')}>1 John 5:7–8</a> (Metzger and Ehrman, pp. 146–7, who say the scribe took the words from the Latin Vulgate). Indian-language Bibles differ here too: the Tamil Old Version prints the longer form; the Malayalam 1910 version does not (<a href={url('guide/base-texts/#what-the-table-shows')}>Two Greek texts</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa">
    <h3>"Why does John 8 have brackets? Is the story not true?"</h3>
    <div class="ans">
      <p>"The brackets do not mean the story is removed. They mean that the story of the woman caught in adultery is not in the earliest manuscripts of John that we have. The papyri 𝔓⁶⁶ and 𝔓⁷⁵, from about 200 or soon after, and the great 4th-century codices go straight from John 7:52 to 8:12. The earliest Greek copy that has the story is Codex Bezae, from about 400 or the 5th century. Some manuscripts put the story in other places, such as after Luke 21:38.</p>
      <p>The UBS editors print it inside double brackets. In their system this means: added later, but very old and important. So most Bibles keep it in its usual place, with brackets or a note, and churches still read and preach it."</p>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">Facts from the <a href={url('guide/examples/john-7-53/')}>John 7:53–8:11 worked example</a> and the glossary entry <a href={g('double-brackets')}>⟦ ⟧</a>. Witnesses: <a href={w('p66')}>𝔓⁶⁶</a>, <a href={w('p75')}>𝔓⁷⁵</a>, <a href={w('bezae')}>Codex Bezae</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <h2>Words to choose with care</h2>
  <ul class="care">
    <li><strong>Describe, do not judge.</strong> Say "Your Bible follows the Textus Receptus here", not "Your Bible is wrong".</li>
    <li><strong>"Not in the earliest manuscripts" is not the same as "false".</strong> Some readers hear it that way. Say what the brackets or the note mean.</li>
    <li><strong>"Some manuscripts" is not a count.</strong> At John 1:18 the NIV's "Some manuscripts" stands for the reading of most Greek manuscripts.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat other views with respect.</strong> Some scholars hold that the Byzantine or Majority text, or the Textus Receptus itself, is closest to the original. People in your churches who hold these views are taking one side of a real debate. Give them a careful answer. See <a href={url('guide/base-texts/#the-case-for-the-tr-and-the-byzantine-text')}>the case for the TR and the Byzantine text</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Point to the decision-makers.</strong> If someone disagrees, tell them that the Review Committee decides, and how they can bring the question to it.</li>
  </ul>

  <aside class="unverified" aria-labelledby="unv">
    <h2 id="unv">Not yet verified</h2>
    <p>These points are kept apart because no source we can name has confirmed them yet. Do not rely on them.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which settings work best for these conversations (a church meeting, a review session, a printed leaflet) and how much detail most church members want. We have no named source on this yet.</li>
      <li>The evidence for Acts 8:37 itself: which manuscripts and early writers have the verse, and how old they are. This site does not yet give a source for that one verse. Check Metzger's <cite>A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament</cite> (2nd ed.) before you speak about the evidence.</li>
      <li>Whether showing readers a photo of a manuscript page helps or confuses them. No source checked.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
</Context>

